  "limits": [
    "The pathway block has only 38 levels and the tissue block only 3, so both are coarse.",
    "The three pools share one training set.",
    "Retrospective; no mechanism, clinical or drug-choice claim; no meta-learning or RSI."
  ],
  "what_this_overturns": {
    "the_claim": "C74 and C80 established that 'the pathway annotation adds decision value beyond drug identity, most strongly when drugs are unseen'. This round splits the annotation block and shows that claim was misattributed.",
    "the_decomposition": {
      "pathway_only_minus_identity": {
        "Synergy": "significant in 0 of 3 pools, and negative in 2 of 3",
        "dEMAX": "significant in 1 of 3"
      },
      "tissue_only_minus_identity": {
        "Synergy": "significant in 2 of 3 pools (+2.50 and +1.25 pp)",
        "dEMAX": "0 of 3"
      }
    },
    "so": "what actually helps beyond drug identity is the cell's TISSUE, not its pathway annotation. Three tissue levels, a metadata field, outperform a 38-level pathway block.",
    "why_it_matters": "the programme's one positive modelling claim shrinks from 'annotation carries complementary information' to 'tissue carries information', which is far less interesting and closer to a known confounder than to a representational insight"
  },
  "the_pattern_one_more_time": "this is the fourth time a headline has been attributed to the wrong component: R99 attributed the increment to annotation when it was granularity; R102 found the marginals were an unshrunk estimate; R119 found the increment reversed under tissue holdout; and now the increment is shown to be tissue itself. In each case the fix was the same: remove one factor at a time."
}